{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"a8365775-9d17-4a80-970f-020cecb4a256","title":"Unit circle and the sine wave"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a8365775-9d17-4a80-970f-020cecb4a256","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A point travels around the unit circle. Its vertical coordinate becomes the height of the sine wave as the angle increases.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"circle_view","region":"left","frame":{"x":[-1.4,1.4],"y":[-1.4,1.4],"axes":"origin","equal_scale":true,"x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"unit_circle","center":[0,0],"r":1},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"start","at":[1,0],"label":"(1, 0)"}]},{"op":"figure","id":"wave_view","region":"right","frame":{"x":[0,6.5],"y":[-1.3,1.3],"axes":"edge","x_label":"θ (radians)","y_label":"sin θ"},"marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"wave_start","at":[0,0],"label":"(0, 0)"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a8365775-9d17-4a80-970f-020cecb4a256","step":{"id":"s2","script":"At pi over three radians, the circle point has height root three over two. Plot that same height above the angle.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"circle_angle","on":"circle_view","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"moving","at":[0.5,0.8660254],"label":"P"},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"moving"},"label":"1"},{"kind":"segment","id":"height","from":[0.5,0],"to":{"at":"moving"},"label":"sin θ"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"moving"},"to":[0,0.8660254],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"center"},"from":{"at":"start"},"to":{"at":"moving"},"r":0.25,"label":"θ"}]},{"op":"figure","id":"wave_sample","on":"wave_view","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"sample","at":[1.04719755,0.8660254]},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.04719755,0],"to":{"at":"sample"},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sample_value","anchor":{"el":"wave_view","side":"below","gap":0.03},"latex":"\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{3},\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a8365775-9d17-4a80-970f-020cecb4a256","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Continuing around the circle traces a smooth sine wave. The top gives one; the bottom gives negative one.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"circle_extrema","on":"circle_view","marks":[{"kind":"point","at":[0,1],"label":"+1"},{"kind":"point","at":[0,-1],"label":"−1"},{"kind":"arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":1.18,"start":10,"end":80,"arrow":true}]},{"op":"figure","id":"draw_wave","on":"wave_view","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"sine_curve","y":"sin(x)","domain":[0,6.283185307]},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"sine_curve","x":1.570796327},"label":"(π/2, 1)"},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"sine_curve","x":4.71238898},"label":"(3π/2, −1)"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why can the sine wave never rise above 1?","expected_gist":"The unit circle's vertical coordinate cannot exceed its radius, 1."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a8365775-9d17-4a80-970f-020cecb4a256","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The height is zero halfway around and after one full turn. Every full turn repeats the same heights, so the period is two pi.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"circle_return","on":"circle_view","marks":[{"kind":"point","at":[-1,0],"label":"(−1, 0)"}]},{"op":"figure","id":"wave_period","on":"wave_view","marks":[{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"sine_curve","x":3.141592654},"label":"π"},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"sine_curve","x":6.283185307},"label":"2π"},{"kind":"measure","from":[0,-1.15],"to":[6.283185307,-1.15],"label":"period 2π"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"period_rule","anchor":{"el":"sample_value","side":"below","gap":0.03},"latex":"\\sin(\\theta+2\\pi)=\\sin\\theta"}],"checkpoint":null}}
